>It has Power and Run lights. Power is on when system is but Run has never
>come on.  It also has an array of switches Power, Halt/Run, and Local 
>Con/Remote con switches.  When a term is pluged into console port and the
>system is turned on the screen will show:

System is probably an LSI-based pdp11 system.

>START
>@

This practically confirms it.  It is what you get from older BDV11
boot roms.

>    	Some guesses are its a multiuser CP/M based machine.  It would be 
>nice to have just running "the Pi".  It will respond with certain commands
>like "0" or "1" and also "/".  We havnt fooled arround with it much but 
>this is what we have come up with. 

Sorry, not CP/M, try RT-11, RSX-11, RSTS/E, Mumps, some older flavor of
BSD unix.

>	If the START prompt means we need an O/S does anyone out there have
>a copy forsale with manuals?  I probley will buy it with a friend because 
>it is so dirt-cheap.  Probley I will just steal the case and use it for my
>rackmount junk laying arround.

The software I mentioned above is all licensed, so you either have to
buy what is currently available, or find someone who will transfer their
license to you.  Since it is an RX02 system (RX02s are 8" floppies which
are double density, or 988 blocks where a block is 512bytes), you will
probably only be able to run RT-11.

I would suggest you open the thing up and check the numbers on the boards
to find out what you really have.  The reason for this is that the boot
message you indicate is something from the boot code, which doesn't check
for a specific processor type.  You could have an 11/03-based machine
(likely), or an 11/23 based machine.

