In article <3dpict$3u7@mark.ucdavis.edu>, ez049336@bullwinkle.ucdavis.edu (ez049336) writes:
>I have been put to the insane task of researching converting our entire
>vax cluster over to unix.  I am aware that we will have to dump the
>hard drives and reformat them and obtain a site license for ultrix or
>something else.  I am wondering is anyone has any experience with this,
>and if so could you recomend ultrix or are there other favorable
>operating systems?  Does any one have prewriten code to deal with
>converting vms files over to unix?
>I'd appreciate any help I can get.

Well, I guess the first question to ask here is, what are the files, and what
are the applications that use them ? There is no inherent problem converting
files from OpenVMS to something else, the big issue is the applications.
A few possible scenarios I can think of:

Software development environment:
The files themselves (.C,.H) will convert almost "as-is". Just watch out for
extra CR's and for "OpenVMS" path's in the #include statments. But will your
code now compile & run ? Do you use OpenVMS specific features ? Compiler
specific features ? How about your MMS/Make files ? What about configuration
management ?

Databases:
Depends on the database. Some vendors give you the capability of converting a
database from one platform to another, sometimes through intermediate files.
Does this option exist at all ?

File server (Pathworks, etc):
Do you have a "server" product with the same functionality on the new O/S ?

As you can see from the short list of questions, the first thing you need to do
is write down what is the functionality that you require from the new O/S, and
only then shop around to see what options you have and as a result what will be
easily converted (aka copying files), what will be re-mapped (aka changing
applications), and what will need to be re-written.

	Hope this helps.

