dtcampbe@indyvax.iupui.edu writes:

>I have this "box" that is the size of a desktop PC and has a a DEC dual
>5.25 disk drive and has the label "VAX Console".

I believe it is a PRO-380.  This box might also have a hard drive (or
one could be installed) of up to 80mb.  It will run P/OS (which you
can get from Decus, and probably somewhere on the net) as well as
RT-11 (which is still licensed software, so you'd have to buy it or
get a license transferred from someone else).  The drives are RX50's and
are 800 block (512 bytes/block) worth of storage.

There may be some vax-console specific hardware in it which you could
*probably* remove (but I can't say for sure as I have never had one to
try out.  I have had, and still have, a pro-380 system running RT-11)

You can also install Ethernet hardware (DECNA), a CP/M option board
(co-processor) and a TMS (Telephone Management System) board if you
have the bus space.

>Exactally what is this box and what good is it.  I have a keyboard for it
>and two different monitors (it was part of a deal) and ALOT OF CABLES.

Shouldn't have too many cables.  The required ones are the video and
keyboard cables as well as a power cord.  The system DOES NOT have a terminal
per se, the software in P/OS and RT-11 emulates a VT-100.

