> In article <D1A30n.28q@info.physics.utoronto.ca>, sysmark@helios.physics.utoronto.ca (Mark Bartelt) writes:
>> | I'm not trying to say that the Rainbow was THE BRILLIANT PC ARCHETECTURE of
>> | the 80's, just that it shoyuldn't be faulted for not being compatable with
>> | what was yet to come. It WAS compatable with everything in its day, that day
>> | just didn't last long...
>> 
>> This is just plain wrong.  The IBM PC came out in 1981 (IBM reported that they
>> sold 35,000 in that year), whereas DEC's PCs weren't announced until May 1982.
>> (In my previous posting, I'd guessed that it was 1983, but I went back and had
>> a look at old DECUS proceedings to verify, and found it was at the spring 1982
>> symposium.)
> 
> Yes, but at that time it was not clear that the only machines that would 
> succeed would be IBM PC clones. There were a number of very interesting
> non-clone MS-DOS machines at that time. The Victor 9000, for instance, not
> only had good graphics but also had a decent sound system.
> 
> Hardware compatibility was _not_ a requirement in the CP/M world, so it was
> not clear that this would be a requirement in the MS-DOS world.

Maybe it wasn't clear to DEC; it was certainly clear to many, many users and to
many, many writers at the time.  People were tired of needing to find and buy a
special version of software for Kaypro vs. Osborne vs. Morrow Designs vs. etc. 
in the CP/M days.  They didn't want to repeat the experience with IBM.  

And EVERY review of a non-PC-clone MS-DOS machine, even if generally positive,
included a caution:  "but it isn't 100% PC compatible..."  This created a
climate where PC compatibility was expected.  Eventually it became a _sine 
qua non_.  Perhaps this wasn't clear when DEC embarked on the Rainbow design,
but by the time the machine hit the streets it was all over. 

