[ me ]

|                       [ ... ]   the Rainbow initially ran CP/M only,
| whereas IBM was blazing new trails with PC-DOS (MS-DOS with an IBM
| label).  Though the concept of MS-DOS as something really spiffy may
| seem laughable today (and, in fact, seemed laughable even back then
| to those of us who'd already been using UNIX for years), it *was* a
| lot better then CP/M, and DEC's original decision to make that their
| "official" operating system for the Rainbow was clearly wrong, from
| the very start.

[ Bob Kaplow ]

| Well, it would have been hard to come put with an IBM PC-compatable Rainbow
| from the start, since the machine preceeded the ibm systems by several
| months! As you say, the Rainbow was technically ahead of its time, with a
| real dual processor 8+16 bit archetecture. Whichever wasn't running your
| application became the i/o front end for the other. The CPM 86/80 operating
| system ran your code on the right chip and handled everything for you.
| 
| Then big blue came along and redefined the "standard" in a different
| direction. DEC had to play catch-up with the Rainbow, but eventually got DOS
| running on the platform.
| 
| I'm not trying to say that the Rainbow was THE BRILLIANT PC ARCHETECTURE of
| the 80's, just that it shoyuldn't be faulted for not being compatable with
| what was yet to come. It WAS compatable with everything in its day, that day
| just didn't last long...

This is just plain wrong.  The IBM PC came out in 1981 (IBM reported that they
sold 35,000 in that year), whereas DEC's PCs weren't announced until May 1982.
(In my previous posting, I'd guessed that it was 1983, but I went back and had
a look at old DECUS proceedings to verify, and found it was at the spring 1982
symposium.)

So IBM clearly had the jump on DEC, by a year or so.  In fact, for anyone who
was at that symposium, it would be hard to think otherwise, since (1) almost
as soon as DEC made their announcements, some people opined that it was a case
of "too little, too late"; (2) at the post-announcement Q&A, people questioned
why the Rainbow wasn't running MS-DOS.

Too bad Digital didn't come out with its products first; PC history might have
been a lot different.  Recall, too, that the May 1982 announcements came as a
bit of a surprise to many people, since it was often reported that K.O. didn't
feel that PCs were a sensible marketing strategy, and that it was only *after*
the IBM PC had taken off so successfully that he changed his mind (but not soon
enough to salvage DEC's niche in the PC market).

