In article <3dil9e$qn4@news.acns.nwu.edu>, jweiss@casbah.acns.nwu.edu (Jerry Weiss) writes:
> BTW did they ever get these chips fixed for RSTS which I believe actually
> used different privilege and user FPU registers?

  Yes. All of the 11/83 components (FPJ11, DCJ11-AE, and KDJ11-B) were even-
tually revised and work fine with RSTS/E now. RSTS/E also check for the FCOs
and will either disable the FPA or refuse to start, depending on which FCOs
are missing.

  Of course, there are still some 11/83 systems with no FPJ11's on 'em because
the Field Service folks said "we'll have to remove your FPA until the FCO is
released" and took the FPJ. When the FCO came out, a different service guy
said "no FPJ, no FCO". It happened to me and to some other folks I know.

