In article <bhunt.788503308@brians.umd.edu>, bhunt@brians.umd.edu 
(Brian R. Hunt) writes:

|>Pardon my ignorance, but how are 128-bit floating operations done
|>under OSF/1?  Some sort of library or a compiler flag I don't know
|>about?

It's done using library routines when you use the REAL*16 type in DEC Fortran.
I don't know offhand if DEC C supports it.  No special compiler flag is
necessary.  You do need current versions of the compiler and operating
system (DEC Fortran V3.5 and OSF/1 V3.0, DEC Fortran V6.2 and OpenVMS V6.1).

|>The phrase "At this time" also piques my interest--is hardware
|>quadruple precision in our future (or is quadruple precision going out
|>of style)?

No, coming back into style is probably more the right phrase.  In the VAX
line, quad-precision float disappeared from the hardware with the advent
of the CMOS-based VAX processor line.  The current generations of Alpha
processors don't have quad-float support, so it's done in software.  (Very
good and fast software, though - not like the old H_float emulation on VAX.)
Will we see quad-float in future Alpha generations?  A lot of that depends on
how much we see people using it now.  It got dropped from VAX because of
perceived low use.  However, it really may not be necessary to do it in
hardware - if you are interested in quad-float, try it out and see what
you think.
-- 

