Oh, dear, just when I thought that Digital was returning to sanity after
wandering in the wilderness for more than a decade, it looks like we're
about to see them repeat one of their historic Big Mistakes.

Some of you may have been at the DECUS symposium (spring '83? I forget)
where DEC announced their personal computer family, via a hoopla-filled
live-by-satellite presentation with Ken Olsen and the project managers
for the three new products.

It soon became clear that what DEC was offering at the time was good
hardware, chained down by abysmally stupid marketing decisions.  For
example (if I recall correctly) the Rainbow initially ran CP/M only,
whereas IBM was blazing new trails with PC-DOS (MS-DOS with an IBM
label).  Though the concept of MS-DOS as something really spiffy may
seem laughable today (and, in fact, seemed laughable even back then
to those of us who'd already been using UNIX for years), it *was* a
lot better then CP/M, and DEC's original decision to make that their
"official" operating system for the Rainbow was clearly wrong, from
the very start.

But there's an even better analogy: the Pro/350.  When it was first
announced, there was only one operating system available for the Pro,
something called P/OS.  (A wry comment made by some people was that
it was aptly named, since it really was a p.o.s. ;-)  During the Q&A
session which followed the product announcements, a number of people
asked "When can we get RT-11 for it?" and "When can we get UNIX for
it?"  The curt reply was "never", the alleged reason being that the
Pro had a number of hardware features which would make it extremely
difficult (one DEC marketroid went so far as to say "impossible") to
port any other operating system to it.

People grumbled.  Not a lot were sold.  Eventually, the Pro series
went off to Failed Products Land.  The really sad thing is that the
Pro (and the Rainbow, for that matter) were nice systems, physically
(i.e. from a hardware engineering point of view); vastly superior to
the junk which IBM was selling.  Yet shortsighted marketing decisions
helped ensure that the product wouldn't succeed.

Of course, DEC eventually *did* provide a version of RT-11 for the Pro,
and Digital (and VenturCom) came out with implementations of UNIX for
it as well (Digital later dropped theirs, realizing that VenturCom's
was better).  But this all happened far too late to rescue what might
otherwise have been a far more successful product.

So the point of this diatribe is twofold:  First, to note with a bit
of mixed amusement and cynicism that Ms. Carr's statement is almost
identical to the wording used to describe what could (and couldn't)
run on the Pro/350 a bit more than a decade ago.  And secondly, to
raise the hope that Digital's Powers That Be might give some thought
to past disasters, and try to avoid repeating past mistakes.

People clearly want OSF/1 and VMS on these systems.  Why not provide it?

