(John Walkup) writes:
>Someone already cleared me up on a terminology
>problem.  I really should have said "Does Alpha
>do quadruple-precision multiplication in hardware?"
>The answer is, of course, no.  I guess no
>computer does. (Except possibly Crays.)

I'm still not sure what you're asking.  Alpha certainly implements
64-bit integer (and floating) multiplication in the microprocessor.
Quadruple precision is a term that generally refers to a 128-bit
floating point format.  At this time, Alpha systems implement
128-bit floating operations in software, but it is remarkably fast.
Some past models of VAX processor (730, 750, 780, 85xx,86xx,87xx,88xx,
9000) implemented 128-bit floating in some combination of microcode and
hardware acceleration.

So - what exactly are you looking for?  How many bits?  Integer or
floating?

