From what I hear, the OpenVMS attrition rate is about 20%, meaning that 
80% of the installed base is sticking with OpenVMS.  A recent Jesse 
Lipcon memo seems to indicate that DEC has reversed the decline in 
OpenVMS revenue.  If OpenVMS revenues were tanking, would DEC soldier on 
with 64-bit support, TNFS, Memory Channel-based clusters, SCSI clusters, 
et al?  Given the firm's limited financial resources, I doubt it.

As for a revival of OpenVMS through better marketing strategies, I don't 
doubt it.  The increased emphasis on OpenVMS at DECUS Anaheim offers one 
proof point in this regard.

Stay tuned.


