In article <1994Dec16.174151.1020@acavax.lynchburg.edu>, teague@acavax.lynchburg.edu (Kipp) writes:
|>
|>I am attempting to connect a modem with a standard RS232 connector (25 pin)
|>to an Ethernet Communications Server which uses Digital's 8-pin connector
|>and I am unable to determine the pin out configuration used by the server
|>for the 8-pin connector.  Can anyone assist?
|>
|>Thanks.
|>--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
|>Kipp Teague                                  E-Mail: teague@acavax.lynchburg.edu
|>Lynchburg College, Lynchburg, Virginia

  I haven't seen an 8-pin connector, but I have seen 6-pin and 9-pin ones.
  I haven't seen the 9-pin variants (DB9-style ) used on terminal servers,
  so I will assume you are refering to the "MMJ" series connector -- these
  look like an RJ11-style telephone connector with the latch and key offset
  to one side.  If so, the 423-style pinout is:

  +-----------------------------------------+
  | 1:DTR 2:TXD+ 3:TXD- 4:RXD- 5:RXD+ 6:DSR |
  +------------------------+             +--+
                           +-------------+

  If memory serves, this is the view looking into the socket of a female
  MMJ connector.  (The BC16E cabling typically contains a pin-flip.)

  Converters are available: one uses a hunk of BC16E cable and an H8571-A
  (female MMJ, female DB25) or H8571-C (female MMJ, male DB25) connector.
  (The DECconnect adapters typically screw directory into the device, and
  the MMJ/BC16E cabling connects the devices.)

  Having worked extensively with both MMJ-style connectors and traditional
  DB25 connectors, I much prefer using the MMJ-based DECconnect system.


  ------------------------------ Opinionative -------------------------------
