In Article <pnord.1137911044C@vunews.valpo.edu>
pnord@exodus.valpo.edu (Paul Nord) writes:
>We've got an interesting problem.  One of our students wrote a VMS fortran
>program to make a database using a direct access file.  If he adds a data
>record with a large number, it creates a nice BIG file.  The only problem is
>that it does not initialize the file.  He gets a BIG file that contains
>other people's mail messages, directory listings, news articles, and many
>lines of junk.
>
>Why doesn't the system clear information from the filespace before it writes
>this monster file?  I can think of lots of reasons that it _should_ zero out
>any data space that it assigns to a user.  I would think that this should be
>taken care of at the processor level.

This is a deliberate configuration choice. By default VMS will clear the
diskspace prior to delivering it to the end user, but this may be disabled by
setting disks to /NOHIGHWATER_MARKING.

The use of NOHIGHWATERMARKING qualifier became common as a performance
enhancement in early VMS releases when the mechanism used to clear the old data
was rather inefficient. VMS now does this very well, but old information dies
hard.

Tell you system manager SET VOLUME/HIGHWATER_MARKING and the data will be
properly cleared. This only need be done once as it sets a bit on the disk to
indicate the use of highwater marking, but there may be SET VOL/NOHIGH commands
is the system startup script.

