In article <3cksuc$so@oac4.hsc.uth.tmc.edu> dct@odin.mda.uth.tmc.edu (David C. Tuttle) writes:
>I've got a DEC 3000 Model 400 Alpha workstation, and I've got the DEC
>firmware upgrade CD-ROM.  What I don't have is the appropriate
>filename to tell the boot monitor on the Alpha to load the upgrade.
>
>The (incomplete) written documentation doesn't tell me, and the online
>documentation is on the CD-ROM itself, which I've been unable to
>either mount or boot from.  So I'm stuck.  Can anyone help me?

David apparently solved his own problem, but since I had to do the
same thing a few days ago, and spent some time searching for the right
document, I figured I would share my results.  The instructions are
in the "DEC 3000 AXP(tm) Systems and TURBOchannel(tm) Options Update
Utility Procedures" manual.  Sorry, I don't have a part number for
this; I didn't get it (or my Alpha, for that matter) via the usual
channels.

Assuming that you are using the latest firmware disk, which has
some version number like 2.9 but contains a set of firmware files
for various different machines, each with its own version number,
this is how to do it:

(1) make sure the magic jumper is in the right place.  I got this
right several years ago, and I just left it like that.  I think this
is now the default.

(2) use ">>show device" to figure out the BOOTDEV name for the CD-ROM
(e.g., "DKB400").

(3) type "boot -fl 0,80 dkb400" (or whatever BOOTDEV name is right)

(4) when prompted for "BOOTFILE:" type
	[DEC3000]M400_V5_1.EXE
to install firmware version V5_1 for a DEC3000 Model 400/600/700 system.

This boots you into the firmware update program, and now you just
type "update" and answer a question or two.  Whatever you do, don't
interrupt this process!  Or trip over the power cord, or have an
earthquake, or anything of that nature.

-Jeff


