In article <3copqo$8j7@zip.eecs.umich.edu>, pete7311@gmi.edu (StRyFe) writes:
> I've got a real quick question for those DEC experts out there.  Our 
> engineering firm just liquidated our aging DECstation 3000's in favor of new 
> AXP's, and basically dumped a lot of misc. hardware.  Consequentely, I 
> acquired a small box (roughly 8.5" x 12" by 3" tall) with the emblem "Remote 
> Services Console".  It has several LED's on front, with labels like Remote, 
> Lock Out, User Port, and Remote User.  It's single most distinguishing 
> feature is a blue plastic key directly below the LED's, switchable to each of 
> the LED's .  Off to one side it has a series of pushbuttons, labeled Baud T 
> and Baud M.  The reverse side is 4 DB25's, 2 male, 2 female.  It bears the 
> Digital part #: 30-25905-01, and the Model # MDS01.  The most curious thing 
> is a large label affixed to the bottom of the box stating "Property of Digital 
> Field Services" and "Not For Sale".  As my friend and I are starting to come 
> into some various VAXEN and other odd DEC hardware, we were wondering what 
> exactly this box is.

It is "Property of Digital Field Services" and "Not For Sale".  F-S will be
wanting to have it back, or to use it with your new gear if you choose.

OK, the RSC is a little goodie that can be set up to give the Remote Diagnosis
Center a reliable link to your console over a phone line, so that when you call
in a problem they can look at your machine even if it won't boot.  The ports
are for your console terminal, your system's console terminal port, the modem,
and a line to a non-console port if you want.  (You can use the "user" position
on the keyswitch to dial into the user port without having to run DDCMP on the
line, if you are that short of dialup ports.)

RSCs are loads of fun to set up.  You need a really basic modem that won't try
to outsmart the box -- I've been told that the following work:

	DEC Scholar, Scholar Plus
	DEC DF112
	Racal-Vadic 1200PA
	Hayes SmartModem 1200

F-S should be able to scare up a copy of EK-KCRSC-FS-002, which will help you
hook it up.  The box is particular about the cables that you use, and I would
always try to use the genuine DEC cables even though they are a bit pricey.

It is probably just as well that the thing doesn't belong to your company,
because there is no use for it that I can think of other than what Field
Service does with it.  Modern ARQ modems make it pretty much obsolete, except
that your average teen hacker would have no idea how to get his PC to talk
DDCMP and crack into your console.  (For goodness' sake keep it in Lock Out or
User when not actively investigating a problem, just in case!)  The other
reason to have it is to get a discount on hardware maintenance if you use it --
at least, we used to, but I don't follow our maintenance negotiations.
