In article <3ceggf$j8l@si-nic.hrz.uni-siegen.de>, engel@numerik.fb6.uni-siegen.de (Michael Engel) writes:
> I recently acquired a DECserver 200/MC and a DECserver 300. 
> So my idea is : why not try to implement TCP/IP on these terminal servers?

  Some newer versions of the DS300 software speak TCP/IP. I think you still
need to load it via MOP (the server boot protocol), though. (Newer servers
like the DS700 can also load via BOOTP).

> As they appear to download some code after resetting, the major problem 
> would be getting the code to the servers.

  DEC sells loaders for MS-DOS as well as the DEC operating systems. You can
get one written in DEC's BASIC (for PDP-11's running RSTS/E) via anonymous
FTP from ftp.spc.edu in [.mop-server]. The protocol description (for MOP, not
LAT) is also available there.

  You'll need the server software as well, and you'll need to buy that from
DEC. Note: many other server vendors make updates, patches, and enhancements
freely available via FTP to anybody. DEC still insists on charging for them,
and often takes *forever* to get patches out for major bugs. Something to
consider if you're buying new servers...

> In other words: is it possible to get LAT information somewhere ? Is it
> well documented or a nondisclosed DEC proprietary protocol ?

  It's proprietary. You can buy a license (and a reference implementation)
from Meridian Technology (they're DEC's licensing agent for LAT). Due to a
mixup, the "LAT Protocol Specification", a "Digital Internal Use Only" man-
ual, was available for sale from DEC a few years ago. Several thousand were
sold 8-)

> I'd really like to use these servers with my Linux machine and my Sun 3/60.

  There's some hope for the DS300 if you get the load image and implement a
loader. The DS200 won't do you any good unless you want to write a LAT im-
plementation for Unix. Trust me - I've done it and it isn't a lot of fun. I
now use DS700's which speak TCP/IP instead.

