    I'm one of the technical support consultants with Digital's Storage 
    Business Unit.  I'll try to explain the problem you're referring to.

    First, it's important to know that the problem only occurs in the RAID
    Array 210 subsystem environment.  No other StorageWorks subsystems are
    affected.  The RAID Array 210 has a feature few RAID systems
    have, which is unprompted rebuild-after-replacement. In other words,
    the user/operator can stick in a replacement drive and just walk away
    without hitting any buttons or keys.  What we've found is that there
    was a design margins problem where a chain of events and interactions
    could lead up to a very small percentage of RAID Array 210 storage
    subsystems not automatically rebuilding.  The pieces involved in the
    chain of circumstances are the controller module fault management
    circuit, the SCSI backplane connector and the disk drive carrier flex
    circuit.  When the problem occurs and the automatic rebuild does not
    take place, then the user/operator has to do the rebuild  manually.
    This has always been possible and is explained in the user manual. But
    now, we recommend that users always check for automatic rebuild after
    a disk failure/replacement, and use the manual procedure if necessary. 

    It took a week or two after Comdex to figure out what was going on with
    the product, but now that we understand the chain of circumstances
    we've been able to break the links, so to speak.  Components are being
    screened to eliminate the tiny percentage that could allow the problem
    to happen.  The screening is being done to shipments from Digital and
    to units at resellers.    We've also published a procedure for
    users/operators so they don't unknowingly operate a RAID Array 210 in a
    non-redundant mode.  We're continuing the analysis to see if other
    precautions are needed. 

    Hope this clarifies what's going on.  If you have some of this product
    or if you support someone who does, get in touch with a Digital
    Customer Services account representative if you need any help.

    Chet




