In article <D0pxJx.3KI@world.std.com>, mew@world.std.com (Michael E Willett) writes:
> With our third party RAID activity in the Digital installed base, we get 
> asked by users about Digital VAX Sim Plus.  Could anyone explain how this 
> works, including pros and cons?

VAXsimPlus is part of the Symptom-Directed Diagnosis kit that is available to
sites with Digital hardware maintenance.  If you drop Digital maintenance, they
come and take it out, or so I'm told.

VAXsim monitors the error logging packets flowing through the system, looking
for known patterns of failure.  When such patterns are recognized, it mails its
diagnosis to a site-designated list of userids.  You get a code describing the
predicted failure of a Field Replaceable Unit, which Field Service can use to
stage repair parts for your site when they schedule the repair.  I understand
that the reports can be delivered to Field Service directly, so that the system
can call for repairs without human intervention if desired.

VAXsimPlus can work with VMS Volume Shadowing to move user data to a hot spare
drive when a failure is predicted.  This has saved our skin on several
occasions, and our users never knew.

>                                  Is it open or proprietary?

An answer as vague as the question would be worthless.  Certainly you will need
a great deal of cooperation with Digital in order to take advantage of it.  If
your product is recognized by VMS as a drive with failure modes similar to
those of your product, you might be able to squeak by without any help, but you
will admit that those are *very* strong conditions.

>                                                              Is it 
> anything worthwhile?

Definitely worthwhile to customers.  You'll have to decide whether the
competitive advantages are worth the cost.  But it costs nothing to call
Digital and find out what it would take.

>                       Assuming it is probably worthwhile, is it important
> or not so important?

The hot-spare stuff is probably not so important to you, since RAID
implementations can easily offer this independently.  The failure prediction
stuff is important to me as a customer, and costly to develop independently.

>                       How does it work vis a vis third party maintenance
> companies?

It doesn't.  See above.

>             Is it available for third-party peripherals?

Only your Digital rep. knows for sure.  Is your product more competitive with
Digital's current product line, or more cooperative?
