In article <D0H8M7.50s@actrix.gen.nz>, dvincent@atlantis.actrix.gen.nz (David Vincent) writes...
>hi
> 
>heres a weenie question. is there any trick to getting a 2100 to
>recognize scsi bus b on a 2100. i've filled up bus a with 4 disks
>and need to add another couple but no combination of terminators or
>jiggling things around gives me a pkb device. is it enabled with the
>eisa configuration utility?

Your question is a little confusing to me as there is not really a fixed
definition for a scsi bus 'b' best I know. Perhaps you are refering to 
the 8 slot storage box which can be in single bus or dual bus mode?

The keyword for that is "TBD: Terminator Bottom Dual bus". 
So make sure the terminator at the back of that box (the 'bigger' plug 
with ICs) is at the bottom, and the connector parked in the top slot. 
The internal flat scsi cable coming from the COMIO board then runs to 
the top 50 pin berg connector (after passing by CD and/or Tape) with 
disks showing up as DKA... Now if you had for example an adaptek controller 
installed, you could run in internal scsi cable from it to the lower
50 pin on the storage box and any disk on an 'even' slot would
become DKB... ( 'b' bus ?). 
Looking at your SHOW CONFIG output, and not knowing off hand, what an 
Intel 82375EB board is, it looks like the next available scsi controller 
would be the MLX075 aka MYLEX ASTRO aka  SWXCR aka DF.... raid controller.
You could run a cable from one of its 3 channels (typically 0 is chosen) 
to the lower connected adn get connectivity that way. You would then have
to run RCU (aka ARCCF aka SWXCRMGR) to configure those disk as JBOD (just
a bunch of disks) or RAID-xx. They would however show up are DRA..., not 'B'.
A rose by an other name...

Hope this helps,		+--------------------------------------+
