a few weeks ago, i posted here complaining that i was ready to buy a
dec machine but that i couldn't get anyone to give me a quote with the
uw discount.  

i received many helpful replies, both from dec employees and other
customers.  thanks to all for your help.  after my post, i found it
*much* easier to get through when i called the local office. :)

---------------------------------------------------------------------

the biggest problems that we had here at the uw were:

1. we thought only one guy in milwaukee (and not anyone at dec direct)
could quote a uw discount price.  we thought that because dec told us
so.  it just ain't true.

2. then we thought that there was also one guy at dec direct who could
also give us quotes (and we couldn't reach him, since he apparently no
longer works in sales).  again, we were told that by dec and it ain't
so.  anyone there can do it.

3. we also didn't know that dec wanted us to contact resellers.  they
never told us directly.

once i got past those problems, things were pretty easy.  once i had
the right information, i was perfectly happy about my dealings with
dec.

the following is a little thing i wrote up for myself and others at
the uw about how to get through to dec.  a lot of it has only local
relevance, but parts may be useful to people in other places.

---------------------------------------------------------------------

These are some UW-specific notes on finding out about and purchasing
DEC equipment.

1. Use the electronic catalog available via the web.  The URL is
http://www.dec.com.  This catalog is best for learning what products
are available, what the performance specs are, etc.  It also has
pictures of many products.

2. Use the electronic catalog available via via telnet.  This is
primarily for getting list prices and detailed descriptions of
arbitrary part numbers.  This is a very useful service, and is
complementary to the web catalog.  The prices don't include the UW
discount.  You can get an account by logging in and following the
instructions; that takes about 10 days.  The address is
order.sales.digital.com.

3. Call 1-800-DIGITAL and ask for quotes with the UW discount.
Contrary to what you may have heard, they can do this.  The UW
discount is about 15% on computers, and less on extras.  The UW
discount number recognized by DEC is 3907.  The Milwaukee office (see
below) may tell you to talk to Mike Dee at DEC Direct (the 1-800
number).  Other people at DEC have told me that he now works for US
Logistics.  Forget about trying to talk to him, and just say that you
want a quote, and offer up the UW discount number.

4. Call the Milwaukee office at 1-414-792-2430 and ask them for
quotes.  Remember, you are buying for the UW, and that means that you
represent a big customer.  Be persistent and polite, and they will get
back to you.  Here are a few people that I communicated with:

Steve Root		(414) 792 - 2430
Mike Eschenbach		(414) 792 - 2517 michael@ecsh.mwo.dec.com
Dave Broadfoot		(414) 792 - 2425
Candice Bielinski	(414) 792 - 0550

5. DEC has a faxback service available at 1-800-723-4431.  I haven't
used it.

6. Contact DEC resellers.  Even though they haven't told us so
directly, DEC would like to see customers buying from DEC resellers.
Here are a few:

Cyberzonics		(414) 241 - 1112
	John Foesch	      825 - 4909 foesch@aol.com

Nth Generation		(619) 451 - 2383
	Jim Russ

Unidata			(916) 362 - 1239
	Karen Stevens
	Linda Parmiter

NEXL			(617) 937 - 0056
	Peter Cavallo

MSI			(800) 947 - 0808
	Bill Rogers

Avnet			(800) 442 - 8638
	Pat Parrish	      837 - 8377

The best way to find out about these is to read Digital News and
Review, a trade rag from DEC.  Of these, I got the best service and
the best price from Cyberzonics.  I haven't actually ordered from
anyone yet, so when I say "best service" I am talking about figuring
out what I wanted and getting quotes.  For UW purchases between $1500
and $10000, one must either get three quotes or buy from a vendor with
a state contract.  Bob Knutson (262 - 6120) is in charge of computer
purchases at the UW, and he told me that the two vendors with state
contracts are DEC and Cyberzonics.  Above $10000, he makes the
decision about which vendor to use.

7. I communicated with other people at DEC after I posted to
comp.sys.dec, complaining that I couldn't get in touch with anyone to
give me a quote with the UW discount.  They seemed to help stir things
up on my behalf.

