In article <1994Dec6.081413.425@flying-disk.com>, frisbie@flying-disk.com (Alan Frisbie) writes:
|> 
|> Mine exhibited the shrinking raster problem a few weeks ago.
|> I turned it off overnight, but it failed to turn on in the morning.
|> Fortunately, I still had the original VR260 in the closet, so all
|> was not lost.

Whether or not you want to fix this will depend on how ambitious you are.
From the symptoms you describe it sounds like the raster shrink was
horizontal rather than vertical (since you say it wouldn't turn on
the next day.)

I'd start looking in the power supply, at the low-voltage rails and the B+
(again, because you say it failed to turn on, e.g. the monitor is now "dead.")
If that all checks out then perhaps the high-voltage supply is dead (in this
case, whatever passes for a power indicator on the front of the monitor
should be lit but you probably won't have heard the typical static crackle
that accompanies the high-voltage coming up to speed.)  Check to see that
the horizontal output transistor is being driven properly and that the B+
is actually making it to the sweep and HV circuits.

If none of this makes a lot of sense to you then you may not want to have
your fingers in the guts of a live monitor.  Not to be arrogant but you
can get knocked silly by these things if you touch the wrong thing at the
wrong time (and sometimes even when they're shut off!)


