In article <1994Dec5.172848.25643@olaf.wellesley.edu>, tim@belle.wellesley.edu (Tim Cantin) writes:

>Has anyone done any tuning on a DECstation 5000/xxx (I have a 120), and
>can they share any experiences with the rest of us?

No, but the following comment are generaly enough they'll apply
to any system.

>Is there any way to get NFS to work faster?

What part?  The protocol for NFS requires writes to a server
to appear synchronous; committed to disk before the write is
acknowledged.  This puts the burden of write speed on the
server and any trivial load to a single disk is likely to
swamp it.  Various solutions to the synchronous write problem
includes:

o  NVRAM on the server with support software, which can fast
   and safe write cache.  If the server crashes between the
   data goes into the NVRAM and it would gone to disk, the
   software flushes the outstanding writes on reboot.

o  Spread the write load over multiple disks.  Since NFS
   serves file systems instead of devices, this can be
   achieved by using an array of some sort.  If the server
   vendor offers Striping and/or Shadowing.  If the server
   is ULTRIX we offer both; Shadowing as a layered product
   and Striping as a ASSET.  If you sales people have any
   questions about Striping, point them at me, since I
   maintain it.

o  Some vendors let you shoot yourself in the foot and allow
   setting a flag on the server that allows writes to be
   asynchronous.

After the synchronous writes the next thing limiting the performance
of NFS is the speed of the disks at the other end of the connection,
the network connection itself and how busy the network is.  Nearly
any DECstation on an idle Ethernet should be able to saturate the
network reading a large file, if the server can keep up.

For other operations the performance probably depends on how many
RPC transactions are required and whether the server is keeping
up.  If the server can't keep up, then retransmissions are required.

>Why does my screen scroll so slow?

You didn't say what graphics adapter you have, but I'd guess it
is either the CX or PX.  As I recall the CX is a dumb color
frame buffer (8 plane) while the PX has some graphics acceleration
features.  The TURBOchannel of the Model 100 series runs at
12.5 Mhz, compared to 25 Mhz for most other systems.  The half
speed bus in combination with the CX makes scrolling very slow.
I think the PX may have been a little better because of the
acceleators, but I don't remember.

The only solution to the problem that I know of is to replace the
CX/PX with the HX smart frame buffer.  Someone in my group got a
Model 100 and he complained of the same problem.  The upgrade to
an HX was a sufficient improvement.
 
>How fast are RZ23L and RZ24 hard drives compared to others?

Compared to other what?  Circa 1991, 3.5" form factor disks
in the 120 - 200 MB capacity range; they probably had performance
to the other disks of the same period, form factor and capacity.
Compared to today's 2+ GB 3.5" disks with media transfer rates
range between 2-4 MB/sec and caches that rival the size of
memory sold with many early VAX 11/780s, they're slow.

Each generation of disk drive is either getting smaller or packing
more sectors in the same space, it is being sold for a slightly
higher price, but better price per megabyte and is faster in at
least of one and often all of the common disk performance metrics.

Compared to their predessors, the RZ23L and RZ24 were probably
faster and cheaper.  Compared to their contemporaries they were
peers.

>Why does my DS run so slow when people tell me the one I have
>should be faster than a Sun Sparc I?

How are you measuring "so slow"?  If your benchmark for performance
is how fast a cat of /etc/termcap will scroll by, then I've explained
part of the likely problem with your system.  The Model 120 was the
slowest of the Model 100 CPUs that we sold (20 Mhz, R3000).  I don't
have the performance listings for it, but the Model 125 (25 Mhz) was
rated at 26.8 SPECmarks (1989 suite) and the Personal DECstation
Model 20 (20 Mhz) at 16.3 SPECmarks.  The Personal DECstation was
slightly slow than the same clock speed Model 100.

Without knowing what you are using a benchmark it is impossible
to say why your DECstation is slower than the SPARC I.  You could
be measureing the weakness of your particular system against the
strength of the SPARC or you could be measuring two things that
aren't fairly compared.

One notable advantage that the SPARC is likely to have is that
most recent versions of SunOS support shared object libraries
and ULTRIX doesn't.  A given application suite can probably run
in measurably less memory on the Sun.  If that different is close
to the available memory of the system, then the ULTRIX system
could be paging and the Sun system not.  Putting more memory
in the DECstation could solve the problem and allow to run its
fastest.

