Paul Bickford (bickford@sphynx.dfci.harvard.edu) wrote:

: Here's a fairly simple question from a newbie owner of a MicroVax II:

: I want to add some more disk space to my machine (don't we all?), but
: have been a little daunted by the prices of RD54 drives (>$300.00 for
: a 'mere' 190mb?). My question is this: are there any 3rd party
: manufacturers of IDE/SCSI controllers for a MicroVax II, and what are

There are plenty of makers of ESDI and SCSI disk (and SCSI tape) controllers.
Emulex, CMD, Andromeda, Dilog (not in business but lots of hardware on the
market).  These controllers run $995 - 1800 new, but many brokers have a 
barrel of them for near give away prices.  The tape backup won't care,
the OS won't care, because the controllers emulate MSCP, do the drives show
up as DUxx.  Unlike the abominable PC, you can have as many MSCP controllers
as you like (within reason) in one system, and transfer an entire disk's
contents from one (small) drive to its new, bigger replacement.  Then you
can pull the now obsolete controller and drive, or keep them in for 
additional data storage.


