In article <3c2dc6$8po@serra.unipi.it>, ferrovia@pimac2.iet.unipi.it (Massimo Macucci) writes:
|>This might be in the FAQ, but I have not been able to retrieve it.
|>A group here has developed applications on hp workstations, which are
|>"different" endian with respect to the Alphas. Now they would like 
|>to switch to the new Alphas, but they have the problem that binary
|>files cannot be exchanged between the hp's and the Alphas due to the 
|>different byte ordering.
|>Does anybody know a good solution for this problem, except for the 
|>obvious one of rewriting the application ?
|>                                         Massimo Macucci                      

  I know of no general purpose conversion tool.  It's easy to code
  the simple case(s), but a tool that handled the general case would
  appear to be a hairy problem in file description specification and
  parsing...

  If working in C on OSF/1, there are htonl() and ntohl() routines,
  and (if I remember correctly) xdr.  HP likely supports both, so
  using the same source code on both platforms appears possible.

  Alpha floating point is little-endian IEEE.  Not sure about HP, but
  I would suspect it's big-endian IEEE format.  (Simple conversion.)

  Conversions can also be avoided (obviously) if ASCII-format files are
  used.

