ncl> show osi transport template default all

and get worried about the typing they have to do but
you could type (at least with DEC OSF/1's ncl):

ncl> sh o t t default a

and get the same result.  However, that isn't a very friendly
command and so the long version is shown as the example.  But
in normal usage, ncl isn't much typing if you use abbreviations.

|>The real problem with NCL, as I see it, is that there is no good way to
|>"browse" a configuration.  You can find the names of the instances of a
|>particular class of entity, but you can't easily find out what classes of
|>entities might exist.  eg you can say
|>
|>	NCL> show routing circuit * name
|>
|>to get the names of the routing circuits, but if you don't know to ask for
|>"routing circuits", there isn't anything other than the on-line help to tell
|>you.  

This is a limitation of the VMS NCL implementation.  With the ULTRIX or
DEC OSF/1 ncl you can do (for instance):

% ncl show routing *

Node 0 Routing Port clnp_port0 
Node 0 Routing Port clnp_port1 
Node 0 Routing Circuit circuit-1 
Node 0 Routing Destination Cache 49::00-04:AA-00-04-00-83-12:00 
Node 0 Routing Destination Cache 49::00-0C:AA-00-04-00-85-30:00 
Node 0 Routing Destination Cache 49::00-24:AA-00-04-00-FC-93:00 

[I've just showed the entities and cut down on the output from NCL.]

The above shows that ncl can wildcard the subentities themselves instead
of just their instances.

|>Nor is the structure of the system obvious from within NCL, or from the 
|>on-line help for that matter.  For example it is not always clear where the
|>names of things have to agree.  These waters are muddied further by DEC's
|>auto-config procedures, which often create entities in several different
|>classes with identical names, which may lead you to think that they HAVE to
|>be the same; they don't always.  

Online help for ncl was first implemented as entity-based (Routing, NSP,
..) but that was changed to being verb based (add, show, create, ...)
due to customer input.  The entity descriptions are still available but
have been put under Module_Descriptions.

