Sorry, my fault.  There is new software in dbx which detects forks in the
debugged process, but I found, much to my chagrin, that it is 
too aggressive at 3.0 and doesn't quite work right in all cases.  Most
of the fixes are already in for the update, and I'll probably put a 
patch together for 3.0 soon, but in the meantime, here's a bit of 
what's going on and what might work.

With 3.0 code was put in to detect forks.  This catches ALL forks the
process makes and it turns out that some of these are happening 
behind the users back and we don't really want to catch all of those.
There is also a behavior inherent in the new support that is unfamiliar
to users and may cause some confusion.  This is how it works.  Dbx detects
the fork and tries to attach to the new process.  At the time dbx detects
it, both the parent and the child are still in the fork code.  Dbx advances
the parent and uses the value returned to open and attach to the child
process.  The child process then is advanced past the fork code and 
then STOPPED. A message is put out to the affect, "New child attached,
use switch to gain access to xxx", where xxx is the process id, and 
switch is a new command to switch between the debugged processes. 
However, if the parent continues, and is waiting on the child, then 
it will basically hang, as the child is in a stopped state.  In
some cases, I've found that the child almost immediately calls exec
and becomes a new process, and dbx does not yet control this so 
it execs and may finish up.  Dbx will not realize this and try to set
a breakpoint but it can't because the process is either dead or dbx does
not have access to that location.

Until a new version is available, you can try using the -noproc switch
to dbx, but that will lose the ability to attach to a running process.
But you can always have 2 windows running dbx, one to run the main
program, and another to do the attaching...



