Barry Kort (bkort@musenet.org) wrote:
: :  I have a Digital VR290-DA monitor which is about 6 years old.
: :  In the past few weeks the picture on the display has been getting
: : smaller.  This morning it was so small as to be unusable.  I swapped
: : it with the monitor from another machine which is working fine.  Is
: : this a problem inherent in DEC monitors?  Do they all go bad after a
: : few year?  Or is this something wrong with this specific monitor? 
: : Has anyone ever come across this before?  Is this something I can get
: : fixed or is the monitor trashed?

: I had some go bad after a lightning storm and power outage.  The picture
: shrank from the bottom.  If I adjust the vertical controls inside the
: unit to expand the vertical deflection, I can buy back some of the
: picture, but a portion of the root window remains undisplayed on the
: screen.  I took a couple of these units apart and examined the vertical
: deflection circuitry and found a can-shaped capacitor that seemed to
: have leaked some rosin filler.  I haven't tried replacing the capacitor
: yet.  I was hoping some more knowledgeable person might confirm this
: failure mode and suggest what parts to replace on the circuit board.

Yes, you are on the right track.  Many video monitors use a large,
electrolytic, NON-POLAR cap in the vertical sweep.  It may be difficult 
to find an exact replacement, but if you look in the electronics catalogs,
IE Newark, Allied, Digi-Key, etc. I think you can find a close equivalent.
These are subjected to intense AC current, and fail in 2-3 years of 
continuous operation.


