In article <5516@niktow.canisius.edu>,
Greg Pavlov <pavlov@niktow.canisius.edu> wrote:
> In article <id.Y05F1.0EC@nmti.com>, peter@nmti.com (Peter da Silva) writes:
> > Well, to start with the Alpha actually runs something like a normal UNIX,
> > while the IBM has this SAA/mainframe stuff in its cold blue heart.

>   Yeah, and the IBM [rs60000] is stable as a rock - almost all of the time -
>   and looks like it has nicely coupled i/o, memory, etc.  Mebbe there's
>   something to this mainframe stuff ?  ... had enough of DEC's UNIX to last
>   a lifetime or two ....

It probably depends on what you know. For people who are used to normal
UNIX and have a bunch of systems out there that already run UNIX and people
who are used to doing things the UNIX way and scripts and procedures tuned
to a UNIX environment, dropping an RS6000 in there is going to hurt.

If you're already using a bunch of RS6000s, that's different. But my own
experience has been that sticking something that's significantly different
to administer than traditional UNIX has a major impact. Even stuff as
supposedly benign as SCO's C2 stuff hurts.

You can (and we do) mix and match Alpha/OSF1, Unisys System V, Intel System V,
SunOS, Solaris, and so on and manage them pretty much transparently with the
same passwords and groups and so on, even the older ones that don't have DNS
or even RFS support. One SCO box was always a sore spot, because you had
to administer its passwords and stuff separately. Unless you plan a homogenous
environment, be wary of stuff like this. Even if you have DNS, and YP, there
is likely to be stuff you need to get at. If you're running UNIX, even if the
details are different in different implementations, they're almost always
documented and visible to the UNIX tools. Change that, and even if it's
theoretically superior but it's not easy to get in and do stuff automagically
(like, you can't add a user except through an interactive program or whatever)
you lose big.
