>whether
>this is the case, I'm just trying to explain what it is I want to
know.)
>
>Point is, we can buy 3000/700's quite a bit cheaper than 200 4/233's.

  Actually, it's pretty simple.  The 3000 series leverages off the
  MIPS based designs for it's peripherals and bus.  So it makes a
  excellent choice when upgrading from a DS5000.  The 3000 series
  is a complete line of workstations from low-end, to high-end.  The
  graphics options available range from the highest performing 2D
  option available from anyone (the ZLX-E*) to mid-range 3D graphics
  (ZLX-M*).  They run VMS and OSF, but *not* NT.

  The 200 series is derived from the high-end PC systems, and uses
  industry standard busses - PCI and ISA.  They can use low-end
  commodity graphics cards, as well as the PCI equivalent of the
  ZLX-E* (the ZLXP-E*).  3D is limited to only the low-end 3D
  abilities of the ZLXP-E*.  Higher-performance 3D is being worked on.
  The 200 runs VMS, OSF *and* Windows NT.

  Both great systems.  Just pick the one that is right for you.

