In <jwright.785999225@amadeus.ucsf.edu>,
jwright@keck.ucsf.edu (Jim Wright) writes:
jwright> Any recommendations for tuning Alpha systems?

In article <CzzA1F.HC0@news.cern.ch>,
danpop@cernapo.cern.ch (Dan Pop) writes:
danpop> Yeah, throw even more memory at it.  When it stops swapping
danpop> madly, you've tuned it :-)

There is actually quite a bit that can be tuned.  In OSF/1 versions
prior to 3.0, you had to edit the system config file or twiddle bits
in kdbx.  With 3.0 and up, most of those things can be configured at
boot time via /etc/sysconfigtab.  Mine for my 32MB 3000/300 running
OSF/1 3.0, optimised for a system with little memory and one user,
looked something like this [*don't* use these for a server!!]:

vm:
	ubc-minpercent = 5
	ubc-borrowpercent = 15
	vm-ubcseqstartpercent = 40
	vm-ubcpagesteal = 32
	vm-page-free-optimal = 96

Docs on what all those mean are in the "System Tuning and Performance
Management Guide" (be sure to read the one that matches your version
of the operating system--there is a *lot* more information in the
later editions).  There are some bugs in the tools for manipulating
sysconfigtab (yes, we've reported them), but once you figure out the
format it is easy enough to edit the file by hand.

Unfortunately, there are some paging anomalies in 3.0 that make things
worse than they should be.  I expect the next version of OSF/1 will be
somewhat better in that respect, but a 32MB system will still page a
fair bit even after tuning.  The ultimate fix is, as Dan Pop said, to
throw more memory at it.

Tuning VMS systems is a whole different art, which some one else will
have to address (I haven't run sysgen in about a decade)...

