In article <D01pIM.L4v@txnews.amd.com>,
Dillon Pyron <pyron@dvorak.amd.com> wrote:
>As a 15+ year VMS guru, and 8 year user of tpu, nuTPU is a much better
>implementation than the BBC EDT+.  In addition, with nuTPU, I was able to
>port my existing tpu code and make it run, with very few changes.

Let's just get the terms straight.  nuTPU is an implementation of DEC's
TPU product.  As such it takes TPU code and has an EVE implementation.
If you have TPU applications, then our product, EDT+, will not help you
at all.

If you are a user who is used to using DEC's *EDT* product (not
necessarily the EDT emulation in TPU) then Boston Business Computing's
product is what you want.  It emulates EDT+ much more closely than any
TPU emulation I'm aware of and it has some extensions that are *similar
to* those of EVE.  It takes EDTINI.EDT files and key assignments.

The bottom line:  nuTPU attempts to emulate DEC's TPU as closely as
possible.  EDT+ attempts to emulate DEC's EDT+ as closely as possible.
If you want to compile TPU section files on UNIX, aSoft produces the
only product that will do this.  If you want true EDT emulation with
the most requested EVE features (column cut/paste, status bar, learn
mode, split windows) my company, Boston Business Computing, is the
company to try.

In fact, DEC has chosen EDT+ (along with our other VMS emulation products)
for inclusion with Alpha OSF/1.
